    fix: tolerate a just-deleted lag in the namespace-leak audit (AUG-080)
    
    A registry listing can lag a DELETE that already landed. Observed 2026-09-25
    (gate.sh, 197/198): the audit reported api: ['auger-pytest-8ff530dda03b'] while
    disk was empty for the same namespace — deleted, rmtree'd, and already confirmed
    gone by the per-namespace re-check inside teardown_namespace (no fixture error
    was raised), yet still listed seconds later; in isolation the same audit passed
    clean in 0.29s. One stale listing failed the whole gate.
    
    Fix is in the audit (tests/conftest.py leftovers), the surface that actually
    false-fired, not a wait in ~200 per-test teardown finalizers: a name still
    listed gets ONE bounded reconciliation — re-list up to LAG_RECHECKS (3) times
    with LAG_RECHECK_BACKOFF_S (2s) between reads, ~6s total, through the module's
    own 429-retrying transport. A re-list, not a per-namespace GET: DuckBrain serves
    no single-namespace read, and the list endpoint is the surface the stale read
    came from. A row that persists past the window is still reported — the audit
    still fails the gate for a real leak. Disk-side reporting is untouched, and no
    deletion retry semantics changed.
    
    Offline tests (FakeDuckBrain-style, in the 429 section's spirit):
    - a stale listing that resolves on one re-check no longer fails the audit
      (wire-level: DELETE 200, finalizer re-check gone, stale audit read, resolved)
    - the reconciliation's timing at the listing seam (one stale re-read, one
      recorded 2s backoff, then resolved)
    - a persistently listed namespace still fails the audit (the tolerance cannot
      clear a real leak; mutation-tested both ways: removing the reconciliation and
      making it permissive each turn the new tests red)
    - the lag budget is bounded (~6s, never minutes)
    - live probe: real namespace created, deleted via delete_namespace, audit green
      in 0.10s
    
    Verified: pytest -k "teardown or survives or 429 or leftover" 11 passed (the
    brief's 7 plus 4 new + 1 re-selected); py_compile clean; ruff check + ruff
    format --check clean on all three touched files.
    
    Co-authored-by: totalwindupflightsystems <totalwindupflightsystems@gmail.com>
